From 4283000b805160445885f0581cbaa33cea4603f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:08:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVDOverlayCodecFFmpeg, settings: PGS made for SDR in an HDR
 video, and the other way round (subtitles.hadesbitmaprange)

Kodi takes a PGS stream as made for HDR (PQ) exactly when its video is PQ. A film file often carries
subtitles of another release: those of the Blu-ray (SDR) with the video of the Ultra HD Blu-ray, or
the other way round. Taken as PQ, the white of SDR subtitles (Y' 235) is the brightest light of PQ,
as bright as the display goes; taken as SDR, the white of PQ subtitles (about 200 cd/m2, Y' 143) is a
grey. Of the two Dolby Vision samples Hades is tested with, remuxes of Ultra HD Blu-rays, the 42 PGS
tracks of one (Tron: Legacy) have their white at Y' 143, 201 cd/m2 in PQ; the track of the other
(Transformers) at Y' 235: as PQ, 10000 cd/m2, and that is how it was drawn.

What a stream is made for is now told by CPgsRange (PgsRange.h). In Automatic, the default, it
starts as its video says, and the palettes of its packets, read before FFmpeg decodes them, tell it
apart, once, for the rest of the stream: a mostly opaque entry with a Y' of 202 or more (0.85 of the
range) is made for SDR (as PQ it would be over 2000 cd/m2; SDR text is white or yellow, Y' 219 and
more); in an SDR video, a neutral brightest entry with a Y' from 115 to 165 is made for HDR (55 to 515
cd/m2 in PQ, where SDR text would be a dark to middle grey). Coloured text, and darker or brighter
text, say nothing. Being told before the palette is decoded, FFmpeg decodes it and every palette
after it with the matrix of the range told (BT.2020 for PQ; for SDR, the video's own, or BT.709 by
the height where the video's is BT.2020): exact, nothing decoded again. kodi.log says what each
stream is taken as, and when its palettes change it.

"Bitmap subtitles made for" (Player > Subtitles, beside their brightness) has Automatic, "Same as the
video" (Kodi's own way: never corrected), SDR and HDR; a fixed choice decodes the palettes with its
own matrix from the start. A stream made for what its video is (the PGS of an Ultra HD Blu-ray with
its video, those of a Blu-ray with theirs) is drawn as before, byte for byte. Disc menus are not
changed (BlurayGraphicsRange.h). Texts in en_gb, es_es, es_ar and es_mx (core 39234-39238, read from
their Estuary twins 32303-32307 by the settings dialog).

Tests (outside the tree): pgs_range_test: SDR text as Blu-ray tools make it (white, yellow, light
grey, greys, green, cyan, red, blue, magenta) in a PQ video and in an SDR one; HDR text at 60 to 500
cd/m2 in both; the real packets of the 42 tracks of Tron: Legacy, each kept HDR in its video and told
HDR in an SDR one, and of the Transformers track, told SDR in its video and kept SDR in an SDR one; a
soft edge says nothing; told once; the choices; a palette among other segments, alone, truncated;
and a mutant of each threshold, of the neutral test, of the alpha, of the once and of the reading of
the segments, each caught. pgs_wiring.py range: the setting, its texts in four languages and the
codec (fails on the tree before).

The choice of SDR or PQ for picture subtitles is CroqueMr's "Bitmap subtitle authoring" (after
avdvplus); telling it from the colours of the subtitles is Hades'.
